Hmmm, I was thinking about this just the other day.  Certainly it's
plausible, given the precedent established by the rogue-a-matic program, but
as we know, nethack requires a considerably richer set of tactics and
strategies to win than the old versions of rogue.

A nice way to approach it, I think, would be to run nethack in a GNU Emacs
terminal emulator buffer, which would provide a nice lisp programming
environment as well as taking care of the grungy details of terminal I/O.
This would allow one to immediately get to some of the more tractable parts
of the hack-a-matic problem, such as automating tactics and providing nicer
interfaces to things such as using bags.

Don't ask me to fully describe how such programs should be coded, as this is
as far as I've thought on the matter.  But I think a productive development
strategy would start on a library of low-level functions for dealing with
specific situations and improving the UI, over which a higher-level
strategic code layer could be implemented.  And in the grand tradition of
nethack, the development would be a public effort, with individuals or
groups of hackers contributing to the entire elisp library.



